From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 03:21:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Pack node storage into a chunked slab of small nodes

Per-node storage dominated wasm memory on large documents: html.spec.whatwg.org has
730k nodes (337k elements, 393k text) and tc39.es/ecma262 420k. This shrinks each node
and the data hanging off it, without changing behaviour:

- Nodes live once in a slab of 1,024-slot chunks, with versions in separate chunks and
  stored once per slot. Growth never copies or over-reserves, so the node-count estimate
  and capacity hint are gone. A node id is checked against the slot's version only at the
  API boundary.
- Tree links (parent, layout parent, children) are u32 slot indexes; slot 0 is "none".
  The slot index sits in the low 27 bits of the flags word; wasm32 memory cannot hold
  2^27 nodes. The layout parent uses two spare bits of the parent link (none, same as the
  DOM parent, or other in a side table). A lone child, the case for 34% of nodes, is
  stored inline in the child-list word.
- ElementData and LayoutData come from typed slot pools instead of a box each.
  Derived layout and paint child lists live in LayoutData, special data in the boxed
  ElementRare, and border, padding and scrollbar sizes in a pooled BoxEdges that exists
  only while one of them is non-zero (75-79% of boxes have none). final_layout() and
  unrounded_layout() return Layout by value; writers go through set_*_layout.
- Text content and attribute values are CompactStrings, inline up to 12 bytes (75% of
  text nodes).
- Stylo's element data cell, its init state and the node flags share one byte, and the
  selector flags, state bits, element bits and constructed display share one atomic
  word, with compile-time fit checks. ElementData is 56 bytes in release wasm32.

Dropping a node also removes it from an anonymous block that wraps it, so no link names a
freed slot. Node fields are read through accessors (id(), parent_id(), children(),
special_data(), ...).

Measured on frozen copies of both pages, with output HTML byte-identical: wasm memory
html.spec.whatwg.org 232.4 -> 170.9 MB, tc39.es/ecma262 148.9 -> 113.0 MB. Load CPU
within run-to-run noise.
